{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The problem involves two lotteries with probabilistic negative outcomes (losses) and no sure outcome (i.e., no 100% certain outcome). Therefore, the reference value is zero, as per the rule. The relevant information for the decision reason is the difference between each outcome and the reference value (zero), which in this case are the outcomes themselves: -4000 and 0 for Lottery B, and -3000 and 0 for Lottery A. The mean difference for each lottery is calculated as the probability-weighted average of these differences. For Lottery A: (0.25 × -3000) + (0.75 × 0) = -750. For Lottery B: (0.20 × -4000) + (0.80 × 0) = -800. The more favorable (i.e., less negative) mean difference is -750, so the decision reason would prefer Lottery A.",
